+# Standard S2: small Rust modules
+
+**Rule.** From [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md): "Target Rust modules under 500 LoC, excluding tests. If a
+file exceeds roughly 800 LoC, add new functionality in a new module instead of extending the
+existing file."
+
+The enforced threshold is **700 lines**, stricter than the "roughly 800" in AGENTS.md: the team
+tightened it on 2026-11-21 (it was 800 when the standard was first enforced).
+
+What is enforced:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Measured | Every `.rs` file under `codex-rs/` |
+| Violation | More than **700 lines** (every line of the file counts, like `wc -l`, plus a last line without a newline). 500 is the target, not a violation |
+| Exempt (never measured) | Test files: anything in a `tests/` directory, files named `tests.rs`, files ending in `_tests.rs` |
+| Ratchet | A file already over 700 lines (recorded in the baseline) may not grow. A file at or under 700 (or a new or renamed file) may not cross 700 |
+| Campaign | `rust-module-size-zero`: progress from the baseline's count of violating files (229 at 700 lines, 2026-11-21) to zero. See [Campaign history](#campaign-history) |
+
+It is scored by [Toolbox](https://github.com/loganvolkers/toolbox) (`toolbox` 0.1.1 and its
+`toolbox-connector-workspace` binary on `PATH`, plus `jq`). Files:
+
+| File | What it is |
+|---|---|
+| `.toolbox/toolbox.yaml` | The repo portal: includes the `file-size` package with this standard's globs and limit |
+| `.toolbox/packages/file-size/` | A vendored copy of Toolbox's `file-size` package (so no network is needed) |
+| `.toolbox/results.json` | The committed baseline (generated; never hand-edit or hand-merge it) |
+| `.github/workflows/rust-module-size.yml` | Runs the Gate on every pull request |
+
+## Commands (run from the repository root)
+
+**Report:** every violating file as JSON, largest first.
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox -o failures | jq '[.[] | select(.scorecard == "rust-module-size") | {file: .fields.path, lines: .fields.lines}] | sort_by(-.lines)'
+```
+
+**Gate:** exits 1 when a violating file grew or a file newly crossed 700 lines, 0 otherwise.
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --out /dev/null
+```
+
+It prints one line per change (on stderr):
+
+- `✗ worse … within-limit evidence grew 370 → 371`: a violating file grew (exit 1).
+- `✗ worse … within-limit now fails` or `… new, at none; new entities must reach within-limit`: a
+  file crossed 700, or a new file arrived over 700 (exit 1).
+- `✓ better … within-limit evidence shrank 370 → 369` or `… within-limit now passes`: a file shrank
+  or dropped to 700 or below (exit 0), followed by `results.json is out of date: run with --update
+  and commit it`. Tighten the baseline then (below).
+- `baseline: matches .toolbox/results.json`: nothing changed.
+
+**Campaign:** progress toward zero violating files.
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json -o json 2>/dev/null | jq '.campaigns["rust-module-size-zero"].sinceStart.entitiesShort | {baseline: .from, current: .now, percentDone}'
+```
+
+`baseline` is the number of violating files when the campaign started (recorded in
+`results.json`), `current` is the number now, `percentDone` is the share of them fixed. The Gate also
+prints a one-line summary, e.g. `campaign rust-module-size-zero since 2026-11-21: entities short
+229 → 228 (0.4% of the way); measure …` (the measure is total lines over the limit).
+
+## Updating the baseline
+
+When the Gate reports `✓ better` (a file shrank or dropped to 700 or below), lock the improvement in so it
+cannot be lost again:
+
+```bash
+toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --update --out /dev/null
+git add .toolbox/results.json
+```
+
+`--update` refuses to write when anything regressed. The campaign's starting count is kept across
+updates, so progress keeps counting from the original baseline. On a merge conflict in
+`results.json`, take the upstream copy and rerun `--update`.
+
+## Campaign history
+
+The campaign's starting point is recorded in `.toolbox/results.json` (`campaigns.rust-module-size-zero`).
+
+| Date | Limit | Start (files over / lines over) | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 2026-11-21 | 800 | 191 / 164,935 | Standard first enforced |
+| 2026-11-21 | 700 | 229 / 185,844 | Restarted on purpose (`--update --accept rust-module-size`) |
+
+Why the restart: tightening the limit moves the goal, so the campaign measures distance to the new
+goal. The 38 files between 701 and 800 lines are new work for the campaign, and each of the 191
+files already over 800 is now 100 lines further from it. Keeping the old start (191 files) would
+have shown the campaign going backwards (-19.9%) on the day the team raised the bar, and a file
+dropping from 750 to 700 lines would never have counted. No progress was lost: the old campaign was
+still at 0% (191 → 191) when it was restarted.
+
+## Changing the standard
+
+All settings are in the `include` of `.toolbox/toolbox.yaml`; commit the manifest and the updated
+`results.json` together.
+
+- **Exempt a file** (measured, but never a violation): add it to `exempt` with a reason, e.g.
+  `exempt: {codex-rs/foo/src/generated.rs: "generated by build.rs"}`, then run the `--update`
+  command above.
+- **Exclude more paths** (never measured, like tests): add a glob to `exclude` (`**` spans
+  directories, `*` doesn't), then `--update`.
+- **Lower the threshold** (e.g. 700 → 500): change `limit`, then record the newly violating files on
+  purpose:
+
+  ```bash
+  toolbox report .toolbox --baseline .toolbox/results.json --update --accept rust-module-size --out /dev/null
+  ```
+
+  The files that now fail, and the files already over that grew by the difference, are listed as
+  `✓ accept`; review them before committing. `--accept` also restarts the campaign from the new
+  state, counted against the new limit; add a row to [Campaign history](#campaign-history).
+- **Raise the threshold:** change `limit` and run `--update`. Nothing blocks this (the package marks
+  `limit` as `tighten: lower`, but `toolbox` 0.1.1 does not enforce it), and the files that drop
+  under the new limit count as campaign progress, so treat it as a deliberate decision and say so
+  in the commit.
+- **Measure other files:** change `files` (globs relative to the repository root).
+
+Check the manifest after any edit with `toolbox validate .toolbox`.
